This paper investigates solutions to the nonlinear elliptic equation
 	$		\left\{
 	\begin{array}{ll}
 	\displaystyle
 	\Delta u + u^{\frac{5}{3}}=\frac{\displaystyle \lambda}{\displaystyle x^{2}+y^{2}} + P\left(\frac{\displaystyle x+iy}{\displaystyle r(u(x,y))}\right),
 	\quad (x,y)\in \mathbb{R}^{2},
 	\end{array}
 	\right.
 	$
 where $r(u(x,y))$ denotes the unique positive value that satisfies $u(x,y)=r(u(x,y))$ up to rotation and scaling. Assuming that the potential function $Q(y)$ is symmetric about the $x$-axis, we prove that there exists a small enough neighborhood around any local minimum of the solution $u(x,y)$, within which no other solution to the given equation exists. Furthermore, this result implies that solutions to the equation can exhibit radial symmetry when the potential function $Q(y)$ is either even or odd. The obtained results also provide a key step in addressing the issue of solutions' blow-up in the context of the given equation. The approach we take involves transforming the original equation into a quasilinear equation, for which the existence and uniqueness of solutions in a local sense have been previously proven. We then establish the uniqueness of solutions in a local context, where which we apply the method of blow-up rates to determine the rate at which solutions to the equation blow up. The derived rate enables us to identify specific areas within which solutions to the equation blow up. The identified areas, coupled with the established symmetry properties, allow us to classify the shape of solutions to the equation within those areas. The results we obtain reveal that solutions to the equation blow up in the shape of an equilateral triangle, with a side length that corresponds to the square root of $16\pi$. The findings also indicate that solutions to the equation exhibit rotational symmetry about any axis that passes through the origin, provided that the potential function $Q(y)$ is either even or odd. The established results also imply that local minimum solutions to the equation are unique, and that there are no solutions to the equation that exhibit symmetry about both the $x$-axis and the $y$-axis. The derived results also provide a key step in addressing the issue of solutions' blow-up in the context

A proposed attention mechanism in neural machine translation, called look-ahead attention, considers future contexts to improve context representation. To prevent data leakage, the proposed method restricts the attention from future sentences to the same sentence. This results in an asynchronous attention model. Even though the model is not optimal, our experiments indicate significant performance gains, amounting to +1.1 BLEU for a strong transformer model on the WMT English-to-German translation task. Furthermore, we observed that feeding the look-ahead attention to simple neural machine translation models can boost their performance by +1.8 BLEU. Our analysis reveals that look-ahead attention is particularly beneficial in situations with limited context. Look-ahead attention may be interpreted as a form of time-aware attention, and our results suggest that allowing a bit of temporal asymmetry in attention can be highly beneficial for neural machine translation. Our code and models are available for public access at this link. https://github.com/ghrua/look-ahead attention-nmt.git} 

