The standard neural machine translation decoding process relies heavily on the distribution over words for the current position conditioned on past tokens. The proposed look-ahead attention technique introduces a breakthrough by considering future contexts to improve the context representation, allowing the model to capture more long-distance dependencies. This is achieved through an asynchronous attention model where future contexts from other sentences are restricted from influencing the current sentence. The experimental results on the WMT English-to-German translation task demonstrate significant performance gains of 1.1 BLEU for a strong transformer model and an additional 1.8 BLEU for simple neural machine translation models when look-ahead attention is fed to the model. The analysis indicates that look-ahead attention is particularly beneficial in limited context situations. The proposed method may be viewed as a time-aware attention mechanism, and the results suggest that allowing a bit of temporal asymmetry in attention can be highly beneficial for neural machine translation. The code and models are available for public access at https://github.com/ghrua/look-ahead attention-nmt.git} 

